WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.637
Start with AWS.

2
00:00:01.637 --> 00:00:06.261
AWS renamed the Route 53
Resolver the Route 53 VPC

3
00:00:06.261 --> 00:00:11.337
Resolver when it introduced
Route 53 Global Resolver.

4
00:00:11.337 --> 00:00:16.438
Several AWS pages still use the older
name, so both appear in runbooks.

5
00:00:16.438 --> 00:00:22.688
Every VPC reaches it at the VPC's base
address plus two, called VPC plus two.

6
00:00:22.688 --> 00:00:27.413
It answers the VPC's own EC2 names
and records in private hosted

7
00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:32.138
zones associated with the VPC,
and it recurses for public names.

8
00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:33.501
One quota matters.

9
00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:38.017
Each network interface may
send 1,024 packets per second

10
00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:42.438
to link-local services, shared
by DNS and other services.

11
00:00:42.438 --> 00:00:45.626
It can't be raised, and
traffic over it is rejected.

12
00:00:45.626 --> 00:00:51.537
That matters to any DNS server on
EC2 that forwards to VPC plus two.

13
00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:56.936
Route 53 Global Resolver is a different
thing, an internet-reachable resolver for

14
00:00:56.936 --> 00:01:02.470
branches and on-premises sites, listed in
30 commercial Regions and not in GovCloud.
